 "task_id": "chem_cascade_budget_150k",
 "arm": "platform",
 "message": "# 768-compound library lab: this can't be done for USD 150,000\n\nNo design from the catalog meets the USD 150,000 all-in budget. The **cheapest LC-MS listed, a Waters ACQUITY UPLC with QDa detector, is about USD 156,700 on its own**. No LC-MS is listed at or below USD 150,000. To put a number on the gap, I built and simulated the lowest-cost design I could from catalog equipment. The results are model-based predictions, not measurements.\n\n## Assumptions I had to make (none confirmed by you)\n- **Target:** 768 compounds per week, meaning 8 \u00d7 96-well reaction blocks (8 \u00d7 12 \u00d7 8 = 768) over 5 days, or **153.6 compounds/day**.\n- **Staffing:** 8 h/day with one chemist, who also carries plates between stations because there's no budget for a robot arm.\n- **Room:** 6 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 3 m with one door. You didn't give room dimensions.\n- **Hazards:** flammable solvents and toxic reagents are present.\n- **Inert atmosphere:** not assumed. If you need it, the cheapest catalog option adds a glovebox at about USD 60,300.\n- **QC and readout:** every product gets LC-MS QC; the screen is a fluorescence readout in 96-well plates.\n- **Compound storage:** the catalog's only automated compound store is about USD 3.16M. A 20-slot plate hotel stands in for short-term storage only.\n- **Not modelled:** a flammable-solvent storage cabinet isn't in the catalog and must be supplied separately.\n\n## Lowest-cost design and its cost\n| Stage | Equipment | USD |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ventilation for reactions and solvent handling | Labconco 6 ft fume hood | 16,560 |\n| Weighing out building-block stocks (manual; the Quantos powder-dosing robot at about USD 72,900 was left out) | Lab bench | 5,530 |\n| Reagent addition for both stages, plate reformatting, assay setup | Opentrons OT-2 liquid handler | 15,950 |\n| Reaction stages 1 and 2 | 2 \u00d7 IKA hotplate stirrers in the hood | 2,560 |\n| Workup filtration and purification (solid-phase extraction) | Biotage PRESSURE+ 96 | 7,834 |\n| Solvent evaporation | Genevac EZ-2 | 52,500 |\n| LC-MS QC | Waters ACQUITY UPLC + QDa | 156,700 |\n| Plate storage, assay incubation, fluorescence readout | Plate hotel, LiCONiC STX44, BMG CLARIOstar | 85,936 |\n| **Total** | | **343,570** |\n\nThat is about 2.3 times the budget, before installation, service and consumables. The catalog's reaction stations that take 96-well blocks cost at least USD 500,000, so I used basic hotplates. The OT-2 doesn't take 96-vial reaction racks, so the reaction format will need checking.\n\n## Simulation results (6 \u00d7 5 m room, 8 h/day)\n- **Throughput:** median 61.3 compounds/day, with a range of 53.5 to 64.3. The target is 153.6, and the modelled probability of reaching it is 0.\n- **Layout:** no violations. The only issue flagged is that plates travel 5.83 m from the evaporator to the liquid handler.\n- **Bottleneck:** this is my reading of the results, not something the simulator flagged. Plates wait longest for the LC-MS: about 11 h on average, with the LC-MS busy 52% of the time. They also wait about 3.8 h for the OT-2. Each plate needs about 5.6 h of LC-MS time (96 samples \u00d7 about 210 s). That figure is an estimate; no source supports it.\n- **Checks:** independent checks confirmed three things: throughput is below target, the cost exceeds the budget (USD 343,570), and the layout has zero violations.\n\n## Why I didn't revise the design\nRaising throughput would mean adding a second LC-MS or liquid handler, a robot arm or a powder-dosing robot. Each of these adds cost to a design that is already USD 193,570 over budget. No two revisions could bring it within USD 150,000 with all the required capabilities, so I stopped here.\n\n## What could make it workable (none of these were simulated)\n1. **Use an LC-MS you already have, or outsource QC.** Taking out the LC-MS still leaves USD 186,870.\n2. **Also use an existing incubator or reader.** Without the LC-MS and the incubator, the total drops to about USD 146,870. That only fits if your assay can run without a dedicated plate incubator.\n3. **Raise the budget to roughly USD 350,000 or more.** This covers one-library-per-week capacity with one chemist, and a second LC-MS or a faster LC-MS method would then be needed to reach the target.\n\nAll durations are catalog or my own estimates with wide ranges. No literature was retrieved to support them. This is not a safety certification: ventilation, solvent storage and fire code need site review.\n\nIf you can confirm your existing equipment, room and real throughput target, I can redesign and rerun the simulation.",
